        <div>
          <h2 class="h-plain">Why this matters</h2>
          <p>When a knowledge system starts to drive decisions, its intake becomes a target. This morning you put a thinking machine to work inside your vault. Now the skill is to keep that arrangement when someone rigs what flows in. Anyone who can put a file in front of your assistant can try three attacks. A vendor can try. A counterpart can try. A stranger whose PDF was forwarded can try. The three attacks are: lie about what a source says, plant two incompatible facts as one record, or address instructions directly to your machine.</p>
          <p>This run stages all three attacks against you at once. They are mixed into a batch of ordinary intake. You know the classes are coming. You do not know which items carry them. The discipline does not care. None of the checks you will build depend on knowing what is wrong in advance. That is why the same checks catch the poison that nobody announces.</p>
          <h3>What you will leave with</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A trusted boundary you wrote down before intake touched it. It names the vault path, the staging path, and the rule that nothing crosses until triage is complete</li>
            <li>Three catches on the record: false citation, field contradiction, and hostile instruction. Each catch has mechanical evidence that a skeptic can re-run</li>
            <li>Containment evidence: the exact targets shown unchanged against P4&#x27;s hash manifest</li>
            <li>A control map that separates path restrictions and approval gates from the truth checks only an operator can make</li>
            <li>An intake rule you can reuse at work, plus a dated P5 transfer seed</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Before you start</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Operator templates present in your Codex app project (<code>DIRECTION_BRIEF.md</code>, <code>OPERATOR_LOG.md</code>)</li>
            <li>Your completed P4 vault plus its scoped external copy at <code>operator/evidence/P4_BASELINE_[TODAY].json</code>; P5 uses the course seed's untouched verifier and this out-of-scope manifest as its trust anchors</li>
