    <section id="ideas" class="home-section">
      <h2>How poison works, and how it is caught</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">Five ideas carry this work. Write them down before anything touches the intake. Each idea decides a move you will make under pressure.</p>

      <h3>Three poisons, three targets</h3>
      <p>Poisoned intake is material that arrives into your knowledge system and carries something designed to change the system, not to inform it. The three classes in today's pack are not three flavors of the same problem. Each class corrupts a different thing you rely on. When you name the class you are looking at, you decide the containment move.</p>
      <p>A <strong>false citation</strong> corrupts your <em>evidence</em>. It is a claim that wears a real-looking reference. After it lands in the vault, your citation trail starts to vouch for a lie. That trail is what made yesterday's answers trustworthy. There are two variants. Today's pack carries both. One variant cites a real source that says the opposite of the claim. The other variant cites a source that does not exist. The move is the same for both. Open the source and compare. Then reject the claim or correct it.</p>
      <p>A <strong>field contradiction</strong> corrupts your <em>facts</em>. Two incompatible assertions arrive as one record. If they merge, your picture of the world is internally inconsistent. Any later question might surface either half with equal confidence. The move is to refuse the merge. Hold both halves with a reconciliation note, or reject the record. Never let the system quietly pick one half.</p>
      <p>A <strong>hostile instruction</strong> corrupts your <em>control</em>. This is prompt injection in plain words. A model reads all the text in front of it. It has no built-in channel that marks which text came from its operator and which text came from a file. Text in data that looks like an order can be treated as an order. The author of a document is trying to become the director of your machine. The move is containment. Never execute the instruction. Isolate the file. Then verify that each named effect is absent.</p>
      <p>If you misname the class, you fix the wrong layer. The classic failure is to treat a hostile instruction as only "wrong content" and then summarize it into the vault. The summary carries the payload. Then the poison sits inside the trusted boundary and wears your own assistant&#x27;s voice.</p>
      <figure class="section-diagram">
        <img src="../assets/blocks/p5/p5-three-poisons.svg" alt="Concept: false citation corrupts your evidence (move: open the source), contradiction corrupts your facts (move: hold or reject), hostile instruction corrupts your control (move: contain and verify no effect)" width="1120" height="220" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </figure>

      <h3>Containment is an absence you can show</h3>
      <p>Containment does not mean that the assistant behaved well. It means that the hostile effect did not happen. That is a claim about files and outputs. It is not a claim about the conversation. A hostile instruction names its own success conditions. Whatever it demands is your checklist. It may demand a changed trusted note. It may demand data exfiltration, which means data sent to a place it must never go. It may demand a deleted record. Go to each place where the effect would have landed and show that the effect did not occur. The note it wanted changed is unchanged. The data it wanted sent appears in no output. The record it wanted deleted still exists. Do not be surprised if a demand names something your vault does not hold. Hostile instructions are written blind to the target. That is a useful tell. Widen the check. Confirm that nothing of that shape appears in the chat or in any output.</p>
      <p>Here is why the chat cannot testify. The channel you would ask is the channel that was attacked. If the instruction had worked, the same compromised process would reassure you. So "I ignored it" carries no information either way. Artifacts carry information. This is also why a baseline matters. You can prove "unchanged" only against hashes that were saved before intake. P4 already produced that manifest. P5 checks it with local code before and after exposure. It does not ask another model to compare folders and call itself independent. A deterministic check that you can rerun separates evidence from a more elaborate "I ignored it."</p>
      <p>Avoid a log verdict that reads "assistant confirmed it ignored the instruction." At work, that is the difference between closing an incident on the suspect&#x27;s statement and closing it on the audit.</p>
      <figure class="section-diagram">
        <img src="../assets/blocks/p5/p5-containment-absence.svg" alt="Concept: each effect a hostile note demands is checked at its target — the vault manifest passes, nothing is sent from chat or outputs, and the named record remains — while the chat's own reassurance is only a claim" width="1120" height="220" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </figure>

      <h3>Catch it mechanically. Do not wait for a confession</h3>
      <p>A mechanical check is a check whose outcome does not depend on the model choosing to tell you. Three checks do almost all the work here. <strong>Quarantine</strong> is structural. Intake lands in a staging area that the vault never reads. Poison cannot touch trusted material because the path does not cross. <strong>Claim-versus-source</strong> is comparative. Before any claim enters the vault, open what it cites and read the line. The check succeeds or fails on the file's content. It does not succeed or fail on anyone's honesty. <strong>Manifest verification</strong> is the catch-all tripwire. Compare every recorded path and hash to P4's saved state. An unapproved change appears no matter how it happened.</p>
      <p>Each check leaves an artifact. The artifact can be a staging listing, a quoted pair of lines, or a saved manifest receipt. "We told the model to be careful with this data" does not establish what happened. The listing, the comparison, and the receipt do.</p>
      <p>One subtle trap is that summarization is not triage. If you ask the assistant to "review the intake," you may get a fluent summary that repeats the false claim in a confident register. That claim is now unmarked and one step closer to trusted. Triage means that you run the named checks. Triage does not mean that you only describe the files.</p>

      <h3>The machine flags; the verdict stays yours</h3>
      <p>Scale the detection. Never scale the verdict. It is fine for the machine to run every check and raise every flag. At volume, that is necessary. But you confirm each catch in person. Open the evidence. Agree the class. Sign the disposition. The triage record is where that confirmation becomes a row with file, class, evidence, and disposition. An unsigned flag is not a catch yet.</p>
      <p>The failure mode has a name. It is a triage system that auto-clears its own alarms. It flags. It investigates itself. It resolves. Then it has quietly taken your job. You discover that later at the vault, when something that "cleared" has landed. Permission modes are the adjacent lesson. A mode changes <em>who reviews an action</em> before it runs. Nothing in any mode reviews whether a <em>claim is true</em>. Action gates can be delegated by policy. Truth verdicts have no mode. They only have you.</p>
      <p>The honest response to overload is to narrow intake or tighten the mechanical filters. Do not let the flags clear themselves and call that efficiency.</p>

      <h3>Match the control to the claim</h3>
      <p>A path restriction controls what the process can reach. An approval gate controls when an action waits for a person. Neither control can decide whether an intake claim is true. Keep those claims separate in the record. The boundary limits possible effects. Citation and contradiction checks establish content quality. The operator owns every disposition.</p>
      <p>One implementation detail is worth carrying into any policy engine. Rule order can change the result. OpenCode's ordered permissions use the last matching rule. If you use that tool at work, inspect the active rule set. Test the exact action you intend to rely on. For this run, the control that matters is the real vault and staging separation and the evidence collected from it.</p>
    </section>

